// Quiz 2: Marketing mix modelling I (one country)
// Generated by assignments/quizzes/convert_quiz.py; one correct answer per question.

$CATEGORY: $course$/Quiz 2\: Marketing mix modelling I (one country)

// question 1
::S02 Q01::In a marketing mix model, "baseline" sales are {
	~sales in the weeks with no advertising at all
	=the sales the model would predict with all media spend set to zero, given price, promotions, seasonality and other controls
	~the average weekly sales over the modelling period
	~the residual of the regression
	####the baseline is the model's prediction without media; it still includes price, promotion, seasonal and trend effects.
}

// question 2
::S02 Q02::Geometric adstock with a decay parameter alpha \= 0.7 means that {
	~70 % of this week's spend is wasted
	=the effect of a week's spend is spread over time, with each later week keeping 70 % of the previous week's effect
	~70 % of sales are caused by advertising
	~the campaign lasts exactly 7 weeks
	####geometric adstock weights are alpha^l for lag l; with alpha 0.7 the effect decays by 30 % per week and the mean lag is 0.7/0.3, about 2.3 weeks.
}

// question 3
::S02 Q03::Which channel would you expect to have the shortest carryover (lowest alpha)? {
	~Television
	~Out-of-home
	=Paid search
	~Sponsorship
	####search captures existing intent and the click happens immediately; brand channels like TV and OOH build memory that decays slowly.
}

// question 4
::S02 Q04::The half-saturation point k of a Hill curve is {
	=the spend level at which the channel delivers half of its maximum effect
	~the spend level at which ROAS equals 0.5
	~half of the maximum observed spend
	~the point where marginal ROAS becomes negative
	####Hill(x) \= x^s / (x^s + k^s) equals 0.5 exactly when x \= k.
}

// question 5
::S02 Q05::Average ROAS and marginal ROAS differ because {
	~average ROAS uses revenue and marginal ROAS uses units
	=on a concave response curve the next euro returns less than the average euro
	~marginal ROAS ignores adstock
	~average ROAS is always smaller
	####with diminishing returns the slope of the curve (marginal) is below the slope of the chord from the origin (average) at every positive spend level.
}

// question 6
::S02 Q06::Media budgets are usually higher before Christmas, when chocolate sales peak anyway. In an MMM without seasonal controls this leads to {
	~underestimated media effects, because the peak is noise
	=overestimated media effects, because the seasonal peak is attributed to media
	~no bias, because OLS is unbiased
	~a lower R² than with controls
	####this is the planning confound; spend and sales rise together for a common reason, and omitting the reason biases the media coefficients upwards.
}

// question 7
::S02 Q07::Why is the adstock decay parameter not estimated by ordinary least squares together with the other coefficients? {
	~It is a categorical variable
	=It enters the model non-linearly, inside the transformation of spend, so it is chosen by grid search or by non-linear or Bayesian estimation
	~OLS can only estimate one parameter per channel
	~Statsmodels does not support it
	####OLS is linear in parameters; alpha sits inside adstock(x, alpha), so we try values on a grid and compare fit (AIC or holdout error).
}

// question 8
::S02 Q08::A Durbin-Watson statistic of 0.9 for an MMM's residuals suggests that {
	~the model fits very well
	=the residuals are positively autocorrelated, so something that moves slowly over time is missing from the model
	~multicollinearity is severe
	~the holdout error will be low
	####DW near 2 means no autocorrelation; values well below 1.5 indicate positive autocorrelation, often a missing trend or mis-specified carryover.
}

// question 9
::S02 Q09::Two saturation specifications fit the data equally well (same R² and holdout MAPE) but give TV ROAS of 1.3 and 2.6. The correct conclusion is {
	~take the average, 1.95
	~choose the higher one, because it fits as well and is better for the budget
	=the data do not identify the saturation shape; the ROAS depends on an assumption that must be justified by business logic, priors or an experiment
	~the model is wrong and should be discarded
	####a very concave curve is almost flat over the observed spend and acts like a second intercept, moving baseline sales into media; fit statistics cannot tell the two apart.
}

// question 10
::S02 Q10::Which channel's ROAS from an MMM should you trust least? {
	~A channel with bursty, flighted spend over three years
	~A channel with large, varying spend
	=A channel that is always on at a nearly constant level
	~A channel with a short carryover
	####a nearly constant regressor is collinear with the intercept; the model cannot separate its effect from the baseline, and its alpha profile is flat.
}
